{"question": "A 45-year-old G5P4105 presents to her gynecologist\u2019s office with six months of increasingly heavy periods. She now soaks one super absorbent tampon every two hours for five days each cycle. Her cycles have also become irregular, with intermenstrual bleeding for the last two months. She now experiences significant dysmenorrhea, requiring 400 mg ibuprofen every four hours for the majority of each menses. In addition, she reports new onset mild dyspareunia with intercourse and a \u201cheavy feeling\u201d in her pelvis. She has also noticed increased urinary frequency but denies bowel changes. The patient has a past medical history of obesity and type II diabetes on metformin. Her last child was born four years ago, and she has had five spontaneous vaginal deliveries. At this office visit, temperature is 98.5\u00b0F (36.9\u00b0C), blood pressure is 137/84 mmHg, pulse is 87/min, and respirations are 14/min. Which of the following physical exam findings is most likely to be present in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Rectouterine septum nodularity", "B": "Globular 10-week sized uterus", "C": "Adnexal mass", "D": "Irregular 14-week sized uterus", "E": "No remarkable physical exam finding"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Gynecology", "Obstetrics", "Endocrinology", "Internal Medicine", "Urology"], "option_domains": ["Gynecology", "Obstetrics"], "question_analyses": {"Gynecology": "The patient's symptoms suggest a condition that is commonly associated with abnormal uterine bleeding and pelvic discomfort, particularly in a woman of her age and reproductive history. The most likely condition being depicted here is **uterine fibroids** or **endometrial hyperplasia**. However, given the symptoms of heavy menstrual bleeding, irregular cycles, intermenstrual bleeding, and dysmenorrhea, **endometrial hyperplasia** or **endometrial polyps** are also strong possibilities, especially considering her age and the presence of obesity and